1 MOUNT

   Mounts a container, a host file holding an ODS-2 volume, on a disk
   device, so that its files can be used: by DCL's commands, and by
   programs through RMS.

   Format

     MOUNT device-name container-file

2 Parameters

 device-name

   The device to mount the volume on, such as DUA1; a colon after it is
   optional.  A logical name is translated to the device it names, and a
   leading underscore (_DUA1:) prevents that.  A controller with no unit
   number (DUA) is its first unit with nothing mounted on it, which the
   %MOUNT-I-MOUNTED message names: with the system volume on DUA0, MOUNT
   DUA "foo.dsk" mounts it on DUA1.  A device govax doesn't know yet is
   made, as a disk.

 container-file

   The container's host file name.  Quote it if the host's file names
   are case sensitive, or if it has blanks.  INITIALIZE/CONTAINER makes
   a new one.

2 Description

   A device that already has a volume mounted must be dismounted before
   another can be mounted on it.

   As VMS's MOUNT does, MOUNT defines the logical name DISK$label in the
   system table (DISK$USERDISK for a volume labeled USERDISK), so the
   volume's files can be named by its label: DISK$USERDISK:[DIR]FILE.
   DISMOUNT deletes it.

   Several processes may use a volume's files at once, by VMS's rules
   for RMS's file sharing and record locking: an open whose access or
   sharing conflicts with another opener's fails with RMS$_FLK, and a
   file deleted while it's open goes when its last opener closes it.

2 Qualifiers
/LOG

      /[NO]LOG

   /LOG (the default) shows the volume's label and the device it was
   mounted on:

     %MOUNT-I-MOUNTED, USERDISK mounted on _DUA1:

   /NOLOG leaves the message out, as in a startup procedure that runs
   each time govax starts; errors are still shown.  SET NOVERBOSE
   leaves it out too.

/WRITE

      /[NO]WRITE

   /WRITE (the default) mounts the volume for reading and writing.
   /NOWRITE mounts it read-only, and creating or writing a file on it
   fails.

2 Examples

   1.$ MOUNT DUA1 disk1.dsk
     %MOUNT-I-MOUNTED, USERDISK mounted on _DUA1:

     Mounts the container disk1.dsk on DUA1.

   2.$ MOUNT/NOWRITE DUA2 "system disk.dsk"

     Mounts a container read-only; its name has a blank, so it is
     quoted.
